  "issue_title": "[bug] Audit random-routing baseline is non-reproducible: shared rng consumed in asyncio-completion order",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\n`scripts/audit_eval.py` is the sealed, run-once \"honest, ungameable\" number. Its random-routing baseline averages 100 seeds, fanning all tasks through `asyncio.gather`. But `_RandomAuditPolicy` holds a single shared `self.rng`, and every concurrently-gathered trajectory draws from it:\n\n```python\ndef decide(self, transcript_text, *, sample=False, rng=None):\n    return self.rng.randrange(self.n_models), self.rng.choice(self._roles)\n```\n\nSo the turn-2+ routing draws are consumed in **network-completion order**, not seed order \u2014 the `random_routing` score varies run to run under a fixed seed. This is the exact issue `trinity.eval.RandomPolicy` was already fixed for (each trajectory gets its own `task_rng(seed, task_id)`); the audit script kept the old shared-rng form and never passes a per-trajectory rng.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n- Expected: a fixed audit seed \u2192 a byte-reproducible `random_routing` number (it's the *sealed, ungameable* result).\n- Actual: it depends on async scheduling, so re-running the audit gives a different random baseline.\n\n**Suggested fix**\n\nMirror `trinity.eval`: have `_RandomAuditPolicy.decide` draw from the per-trajectory `rng` that `run_trajectory` passes through (instance rng only as a fallback), and pass `rng=task_rng(seed_s, t.task_id)` per trajectory in the audit's random loop.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python 3.12; commit `c23c767` (current `main`).",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-10T19:09:53.000Z",
